content.text:
It's fine as-is. Nested try-catches are valid and idiomatic C++ when you have two independent operations that can each fail and need different recovery strategies. The outer `try` handles "couldn't resolve argv[0]", the inner `try` handles "couldn't resolve current_path either."

The only alternative would be to pull the fallback into a separate helper function with its own try-catch, but that adds indirection for no real benefit — the logic here is clear and self-contained.

The one thing worth noting: the inner `try` only catches `filesystem_error`. If `find_repo_root` ever threw a different exception type (e.g. `std::bad_alloc` under memory pressure), it would still propagate out uncaught. That's probably fine for this use case, but if you want to be exhaustive:

```cpp
} catch (const std::exception& fallback_error) {
    std::cerr << "Error: could not resolve current_path either: " << fallback_error.what() << "\n";
    return 1;
}
```

Whether that's overkill depends on how defensive you want the code to be.
